[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This is OPNsense, the open-source firewall and routing platform that gives you the features of expensive commercial firewalls free on hardware you choose. Stateful firewalling, intrusion detection, WireGuard and OpenVPN, traffic shaping, DNS filtering behind a'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'genuinely modern web interface on a FreeBSD foundation that\'s been hardened for 12 years. Last week on this channel we freed a router with OpenWRT. Today we guard the whole network. Same spirit,'

[00:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bigger wall. The feature wall reads like a commercial data sheet with the price crossed out. A stateful packet filter firewall with aliases and rule tracking, intrusion detection and prevention through Suricata, VPN every way you\'d'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want it. WireGuard with QR code client setup, OpenVPN and IPsec for site-to-site. Unbound DNS with blocklist filtering so ad and malware domains die at the edge. Traffic shaping, captive'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'portals for guest networks, high availability failover with a second box and multi-WAN if you carry two internet lines. Every bit of it configured from the web interface and everything the GUI does the API can do too. Rules carry'

[01:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'persistent labels for tracking and aliases keep big rule sets readable, details that matter at rule 50. OPNsense is stewarded by Deciso, a Dutch network hardware company that forked the project'

[01:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into life in 2015 and has funded its development since. The classic sustainable model. They sell appliances and support, the software stays free and BSD licensed. The two lead developers, Franco Ficner'

[01:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and Ad Schellevis, have nearly 18,000 commits between them on the core repository alone. 11 years of the same two names at the top of the log is its own kind of security audit. Put a number on that'

[01:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'continuity. 17,572 commits from just the top two maintainers on a core repo carrying 4,777 stars and nearly a thousand forks. Firewall users don\'t hand out stars the'

[01:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'way app users do, they hand out uptime. The install base lives in home labs, offices, and data center edges quietly, which is exactly where a firewall belongs. The forum and subreddit run'

[02:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'deep on every deployment question. The community\'s answer archive is part of the product. The current release is 26.7, code named Xenial Xerops. The releases are named alphabetically after'

[02:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'animals, and yes, they had to find an animal starting with X. Under the whimsy is serious modernization. FreeBSD 15.1, OpenSSL 3.5, PHP 8.5, and Python 3.13. Firewall rules now on the new MVC and'

[02:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'API framework by default, a migration assistant moving outbound NAT to the new source NAT architecture, and captive portal IPv6. The pattern matters more than any single'

[02:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'item. Two scheduled releases a year, January and July, hot fixes in between. 26.7.3 landed in late August, and the whole change log published for reading. Deployment is classic appliance'

[03:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'building. Download the installer image, write it to USB, boot a box with two network ports, a retired desktop with a second NIC, a fanless mini PC from the usual suspects, or Deciso\'s own hardware'

[03:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'if you want to fund the project. The installer walks you through disk and interfaces. You land on the web dashboard at 192.168.1.1. Run the setup wizard, and you have a'

[03:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'real firewall. From there, every feature in this video is a checkbox and a rule away. Old ThinkCenters and fanless N100 boxes have become the community\'s favorite donor hardware. Sub $150 firewalls are routine. The comparison'

[03:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everyone asks about, pfSense. Same 2000s lineage. Both descend from m0n0wall, and OPNsense forked in 2015 over openness and governance.'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The honest differences today, OPNsense ships two fixed releases a year with a public roadmap, pushed its interface into a modern MVC framework with a full API, and keeps everything BSD licensed with development in the'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'open. pfSense remains a capable product with a bigger legacy install base and its own commercial editions. Both will firewall your network well. The philosophical difference is who steers and how visibly. What daily life'

[04:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'looks like once it\'s in place, every device in the house behind real ingress rules, the smart TV and the robot vacuum exiled to an IoT VLAN that can reach the internet but not your laptop. WireGuard on your phone so a coffee shop'

[04:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'connection tunnels home before it touches anything, ads and trackers filtered at DNS for every device at once, and dashboards that finally answer which device is eating the bandwidth with graphs not guesses. The website and'

[04:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'docs hold up on a phone. Release notes, the full documentation site, and an active forum for the inevitable how do I VLAN questions. Worth knowing the ecosystem, too. A plugin system extends the core from'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'CrowdSec to Zabbix agents to ACME certificates. And the business edition exists for companies that want a support contract, funding the free one you\'ll actually run. The plugin list installs from inside the'

[05:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'GUI, so extending the box never means leaving the browser. The health readout, 4,657 stars on core, 987 forks, 299 open issues on an actively triaged tracker,'

[05:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and 22 repositories across the organization. Think core, plugins, docs, tools, all public. 12 years, 24 major releases, zero missed cadences. Boring in the way that critical infrastructure'

[05:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'should be. The honest context. Licensing is clean. BSD two-clause, as open as it gets with a commercial company sustainably behind it. The real considerations are operational. A dedicated firewall means dedicated'

[05:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hardware. Cheap, but not zero, and it wants two network ports and a little power draw around the clock. There\'s a learning curve. VLANs, NAT, and rule ordering are networking concepts, not app settings. Budget a weekend and lean'

[05:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on the forum. You become your own firewall vendor. Updates are one click, but clicking them is now your job. And know thyself. If all you want is ad blocking, a Pi-hole is less machinery. OPNsense is for when you want the whole'

[06:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'edge of your network to answer to you. Your network has exactly one front door, and something is guarding it right now. The question is whether you chose it, and whether you can read its source. OPNsense makes the answer yes on both'

[06:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'counts for free. Website, docs, and the GitHub organization are linked in the description. If this earns a spot between your modem and your switch, subscribe. We showcase an open-source project every single week. See you in'

[06:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the next one.'
